is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No fault is present. The OSPF virtual-link between r4 and r8 is currently up and carrying backbone (Area 0) adjacency across Area 1 as designed. Evidence:
- On r4, OSPF neighbor detail shows “Neighbor 18.1.4.1 … In the area 0.0.0.0 via interface VLINK0 … State is Full” using local IP 45.1.1.1.
- On r8, OSPF neighbor detail shows “Neighbor 14.1.4.1 … In the area 0.0.0.0 via interface VLINK0 … State is Full” using local IP 58.1.1.2.
- Both routers’ OSPF process reports “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 1” under Area 1, confirming an established virtual-link.
- The FRR configs explicitly include the reciprocal virtual-link statements: r4 “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1” and r8 “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1.”
- Backbone routers learn Area 2 networks: for example, r3 installs OSPF routes to 80.0.0.0/8 via r4 (34.1.1.2), and r5 learns 80.0.0.0/8 via r8 (58.1.1.2), showing area reachability end-to-end. Host-to-host pings (pc0↔pc12) succeed with 0% loss.

Mechanism: The virtual-link creates a logical Area 0 adjacency between r4 and r8 across Area 1; since both ends are Full on VLINK0, summary LSAs from Area 2 are propagated into the backbone and vice versa. Localization: FRR ospfd on r4 and r8, interface VLINK0. Root cause: No current outage—any observed “collapse” was likely a transient during router reload/convergence. Corrective fix: None required; monitor. If desired, ensure ospfd auto-starts on boot and keep the Area 1 transit interfaces (45.1.1.0/24, 58.1.1.0/24) non-passive to avoid future flaps.
=== END ===